+In principle the full multiplicative series could define a global Bargmann
+state only after its moving zeros and growth are controlled.  A finite
+exponential truncation cannot: cumulant poles at zeros of $Y_0$ become
+essential singularities, while a pure exponential is zero-free.  A global
+form would likely separate a zero-carrying canonical product from its
+exponential factor.  Direct multiple scales or quasilinearization of the
+logarithmic-derivative Riccati equation are plausible local or sectorial
+directions, but moving poles, Stokes data, and Bargmann admissibility leave
+their practical closure open.  This is not a closed global hierarchy, and
+the Bessel rather than Weber description remains sectorial and zero-dependent.
 
